edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193648 #edb/ref 1061 41650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f42bb3f4-1e7f-53f5-ad85-ca2e942d30b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193649 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193649 #edb/ref 1061 41653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193649 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c579fcb-af17-53b9-89c4-b7c4d86d6a8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193649 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193650 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193650 #edb/ref 1061 41665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193650 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "68866355-7603-545f-b26a-1a0cee48c958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193650 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193651 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193651 #edb/ref 1061 41670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18e8a405-fc8d-5344-9133-d42b106e46e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193652 #edb/ref 1061 41676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40d83f58-34e5-5b7b-b300-54ae33e99a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1061 41677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fda08207-59ef-5481-816d-d4abed842b3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193876 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that $H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nIn the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ there are two **trivial subspaces**.\n\n- One trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}\n$$\n\n consisting of just the zero vector. This is a subspace because the sum of two zero vectors is the zero vector, and any multiple of the zero vector is the zero vector.\n\n- Another trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH = \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\n consisting of the entire set $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is a subspace because the sum of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ and any multiple of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193876 #edb/ref 1065 6854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193876 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "01ed4d92-df35-5f3c-aa41-43246f6ea9e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193876 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193877 #edb/ref 1064 "In $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are straight lines that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows a subspace in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c0ea9584ae3229a7930c6ef2b53031770a7190b980b5b595cc233cae29ea49d.png)\n\nLet's now consider a few examples of subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that are *not* subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5aac9808e9196653ee54974b495443a95d8913cd0b220a6893acb68ff3b967bb.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is *not* a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II is not a subspace because $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$ belong to the set while $\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under vector addition.\n\n- Diagram III is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $-\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193877 #edb/ref 1065 6857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193877 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "33c42a7a-a5c2-5b36-a839-4fab6351c9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193877 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193878 #edb/ref 1064 "In $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ are straight lines or planes that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/57/57b6625b607537d1c90e849d6ee6ac8480648aa4ad6e3a357bedc8d8408256ad.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the sets shown in the diagrams below are *not* subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4fbaee302e1c25e740f4164841a674243a43197b0f20ddb35d2d41bb4175b68.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a plane that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193878 #edb/ref 1065 6861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193878 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8d9802f0-c392-5e37-afb4-72f73451813e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193878 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193879 #edb/ref 1064 "Lastly, consider the set $\\mathbb{R},$ which belongs to the family of vector spaces $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (when $n=1$). This vector space has only two subspaces, both of which are trivial:\n\n- the zero subspace $\\{0\\},$ which contains only the number $0,$ and\n\n- the whole space $\\mathbb{R}$ (or, formally, $\\mathbb{R}^1$), which contains all the real numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193879 #edb/ref 1065 6862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193879 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb0671eb-0c5d-59dc-a3f9-cda72b4da034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193879 #edb/ref 1067 "Final Notes About the Set of Real Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247121 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462568 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186247121 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462569 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186247121 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186462570 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186247121 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110449" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247121 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247121 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8c/8c695ea0cb09807c22fa655182945e838a7c5e454680176add27748945d143e5.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186247121 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a60d4465-319d-5ba9-93b8-d257af2c79a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247121 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465204 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465205 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465206 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275441 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/00/003b4f77a413444989bcec8b9682465419a58ded55ed95cefb4f43c122bdc3d3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0609c9a8-63cb-5946-854d-790ce4ad14b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275441 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465210 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465211 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465212 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158102" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275443 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/42b23af4df7892df46461b01fd549ca56723dde0c08d3e88b71ea119bf45ba23.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186275443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec726d7a-9c79-5a1b-91e2-01dc3e4c2650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275443 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But e